  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Create the serial structural candidate: enumerate boundary pairs on S=min(M,N), scan L=max(M,N), and store input as data[S][L] (transpose while reading when columns are S). For each start boundary, increment a private compressed L-vector as the end expands, then invoke a 1-D counting kernel. This removes the fixed O(M*N^2) orientation and makes update scans contiguous. Keep negative-value support and 64-bit answers; validate 1-by-N, N-by-1, and rectangular cases.",
        "B": "Own parallel decomposition of the compressed-band algorithm. Parallelize independent start boundaries with a persistent OpenMP parallel region, dynamic/guided small chunks to balance the triangular work, thread-private accumulator/hash contexts, and a 64-bit reduction. Do not share maps or force more than the available four cores. Measure serial versus parallel variants under the existing build/runtime setup; retain a safe serial path if permitted OpenMP support is unavailable.",
        "C": "Own the 1-D counting and low-level path. Benchmark a fixed-capacity, epoch-stamped open-address table against reserved unordered_map: at most L+1 prefix keys exist, but use robust hashing for negative/patterned sums and preserve prefix order. A full frequency domain is unsafe because sums approach about +/-998001000; investigate a dense table only behind a proven bounded-range fallback. Also assess disabled iostream synchronization, the universal |k| > 1000*M*N rejection, and an adaptive nonnegative-matrix kernel only with a correct generic fallback (especially k=0 and zeros).",
        "G": "Integrate only measured valid outputs from A/B/C. Compare baseline, oriented serial, kernel-only, parallel-only, and combined candidates using the existing oracle and repeated automatic measurements; gains are not assumed additive. Preserve stdin/stdout semantics, signed inputs, all valid dimensions, and 64-bit result handling. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler selection, target, or budgets; select the fastest correct single program, with the baseline-compatible serial option if parallel integration regresses.",
        "analysis": "The valid baseline averages 0.251865 s on the 4-core ARM64 target. It is serial, always squares N in O(M*N^2), scans strided row-prefix data, and performs costly unordered_map operations for every column band. The main safe opportunities are dimension-adaptive contiguous compression, a lower-overhead prefix-frequency kernel, fast input, and coarse-grained independent-band parallelism. All branches must remain correct for negative entries; nonnegative-only techniques require runtime proof and fallback."
      },
